PLACES=[
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Michigan
N('eastern-michigan-asylum-site-pontiac-mi','Eastern Michigan Asylum site (Pontiac State Hospital, Clinton Valley Center)','Pontiac','mi','inst','gone',42.64547,-83.32239,'site','view','drive','','1873–1997',
  "Michigan's second asylum, voted in 1873 to relieve the crowded hospital at Kalamazoo; Pontiac won the bidding over Detroit and other towns. Elijah E. Myers, the architect of the Michigan State Capitol, designed the main building on the Kirkbride plan, and it opened on August 1, 1878, with 222 patients under its first superintendent, Henry Mills Hurd. It was enlarged again and again (1882 to 1895, 1906, 1914 and 1938), was named Pontiac State Hospital in 1911 and Clinton Valley Center in 1973, and held about 3,100 patients at its peak in the 1950s. The state closed it in 1997 and the buildings were pulled down in 2000.",
  "Nothing of the hospital is described as standing: Wikipedia says a housing subdivision now covers the site. The main building of 1878 was three and a half storeys of red brick with a steep slate roof and several towers, a centre for offices with a wing for each sex; the campus had 44 structures in all, among them a chapel of 1907 by Smith, Hinchman and Grylls. The National Register district (1981) was cut back in 1986.",
  '','140 Elizabeth Lake Road, now private housing: look from the public street only.',
  [W('Clinton Valley Center'),NR('81000315','Eastern Michigan Asylum Historic District')],
  brick='?',arch='Elijah E. Myers',archw="Wikipedia: 'The architect for the original main hospital was Elijah E. Myers', who also drew the additions of 1882 to 1895"),
N('eloise-kay-beard-building-westland-mi','Eloise, Kay Beard Building (Wayne County Poor House and Asylum)','Westland','mi','inst','standing',42.28724,-83.34266,'site','open','drive','','1839–1986',
  "Wayne County's poorhouse moved in 1839 to 280 acres in Nankin Township, where a log tavern became the keeper's house, and grew into Eloise: poorhouse, psychiatric hospital and general hospital on one site, the only county in Michigan that ran all three (Wikipedia). It had its own farm, police and fire departments, railroad, bakery and power plant, and at its peak in the Great Depression about 10,000 people lived there. The psychiatric division began closing in 1977, the last psychiatric patients left in 1982 when the state took over, and the general hospital closed in 1986. The Kay Beard Building, the 'D' Building of 1931 that took psychiatric admissions, is the best-known survivor; Wayne County used it for offices until 2016 and then sold it with the other remaining property to a developer for $1.",
  "A five-storey red brick building (AP, 2026). It now holds a haunted attraction, Eloise Asylum, with paranormal tours of the upper floors and overnight stays; families with ties to the hospital have objected to the use. Also left, says Wikipedia: the old commissary, used as a family homeless shelter, and the firehouse (once the laundry) and power plant, the power plant slated for demolition in 2025. The bakery burned in 2016 and the smokestack with its brick lettering was pulled down in 2006. A Michigan historical marker stands outside; the rest of the grounds are a strip mall, a golf course and condominiums.",
  '','30712 Michigan Avenue. Privately owned; the attraction and tours run seasonally, mostly round Halloween. When it opens was not checked on its own page. The Eloise Cemetery is a separate place.',
  [W('Eloise (psychiatric hospital)'),['AP: tours of a former psychiatric hospital (October 2026)','https://www.ajc.com/news/2026/10/tours-offer-halloween-enthusiasts-visits-to-former-psychiatric-hospital-and-funeral-home/'],['WWJ (Audacy): the real story of Eloise','https://www.audacy.com/wwjnewsradio/news/local/the-real-story-of-westlands-eloise-psychiatric-hospital']],
  brick='yes',bcol='red',hours='?'),
N('newberry-state-hospital-mi','Newberry State Hospital (Upper Peninsula Asylum for the Insane)','Newberry','mi','inst','standing',46.3314,-85.50779,'site','view','drive','','1893–1992',
  "The state voted an asylum for the Upper Peninsula in 1893; building began that year and the first patients came in November 1895. It was laid out on the pavilion plan: some twenty buildings round a central quadrangle of about 11 acres, joined by a covered walk, with a nurses' home, a tuberculosis cottage and farm buildings outside and a farm of about 720 acres. It was named Newberry State Hospital in 1911 and held over 1,000 patients in 1920. Asylum Projects and WKFR say it closed as a hospital in 1992, SAH Archipedia says 1995; the state opened the Newberry Correctional Facility on the grounds in 1996.",
  "Georgian Revival buildings of red brick (SAH Archipedia), several of which serve the prison. Cottages G to L were pulled down in the 1970s and Cottage N after its roof fell in; in 2020 and early 2021 the state took down the superintendent's house and the nurses' home, and six more were to go in 2021: the Ferguson building, the amusement hall, the shipping warehouse and a quadrangle cottage among them (Newberry News). Which old buildings still stand was not checked.",
  '','An active state prison of the Michigan Department of Corrections, on Newberry Avenue (M-123) south of the village. Posted no trespassing (WKFR): look from the public road only.',
  [['SAH Archipedia: Newberry Hospital for the Insane','https://sah-archipedia.org/node/4891'],[AP+'Newberry State Hospital','https://asylumprojects.org/index.php/Newberry_State_Hospital'],['Newberry News: old state hospital buildings coming down (2021)','https://mynewberrynews.com/community/old-state-hospital-buildings-coming-down/'],['WKFR: the asylum in the U.P. (2023)','https://wkfr.com/newberry-state-hospital/'],['Michigan Department of Corrections: Newberry Correctional Facility','https://www.michigan.gov/corrections/prisons/newberry-correctional-facility']],
  conf='mixed',brick='yes',bcol='red',arch='Charlton, Gilbert and Demar',archw="SAH Archipedia names Charlton, Gilbert, and Demar as architects, 'along with others'"),
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Wisconsin
N('mendota-state-hospital-madison-wi','Mendota State Hospital (Wisconsin Hospital for the Insane) and the Wisconsin Memorial Hospital','Madison','wi','inst','standing',43.1320,-89.4013,'area','view','drive','','1860–',
  "Wisconsin's first mental hospital, on land bought from former governor Leonard J. Farwell on the north shore of Lake Mendota. Stephen Vaughn Shipman designed it on the Kirkbride plan; it opened on July 14, 1860, the west wing followed by 1862, and by 1881 it was 569 feet long. It was renamed Mendota State Hospital in 1935 and Mendota Mental Health Institute in 1974, and is still a state hospital. New buildings replaced the old one from the 1950s, and the Kirkbride building was pulled down at a date the sources do not give. Next to it, on Farwell's Point, the state built the Wisconsin Memorial Hospital in 1921 and 1922 for veterans of the Great War with mental disorders, the first arrangement of its kind between a state and the federal government; it was leased to the Veterans Administration in 1937 and later became part of Mendota.",
  "The Shipman building is gone. What is old and standing is the Memorial Hospital group (National Register 1988): the Neo-Classical brick main building of 1921 and 1922, with stone-capped brick pilasters and garlanded stone plaques on the parapet, and a dozen buildings of 1922 to 1932 round it, cottages in a Craftsman style with Tudor touches, a chapel, a recreation hall and the superintendent's house of 1932. One cottage was pulled down in 1981.",
  '','An active state psychiatric hospital of the Wisconsin Department of Health Services, 301 Troy Drive; the Memorial Hospital group is at 816 Troy Drive. Look from the public road only.',
  [W('Mendota Mental Health Institute'),NR('88002183','Wisconsin Memorial Hospital Historic District'),[KB+'Mendota State Hospital','https://www.kirkbridebuildings.com/buildings/mendota/']],
  brick='?',arch='Stephen Vaughn Shipman',archw="Wikipedia: architect Stephen Vaughn Shipman was commissioned to design the facility on the Kirkbride plan (the main building, now gone); the Register form gives no architect for the Memorial Hospital"),
N('wisconsin-home-feeble-minded-chippewa-falls-wi','Wisconsin Home for the Feeble-Minded (Northern Wisconsin Center)','Chippewa Falls','wi','inst','standing',44.93097,-91.35993,'area','view','drive','','1896–',
  "Wisconsin's first state institution for people with developmental disabilities, voted in 1895 and opened three miles east of Chippewa Falls in June 1897 (June 16 in the National Register form for the Southern Wisconsin Home, June 17 on the state marker) as the Wisconsin Home for the Feeble-Minded. It began with room for 250 and grew to about 1,000 beds by 1934, with an administration building, two schoolhouses, a hospital, shops, dairy barns and many cottages. It was renamed the Northern Wisconsin Colony and Training School in 1923 and the Northern Wisconsin Center for the Developmentally Disabled in 1976, when it turned towards care in the community.",
  "The state's survey (1993) found Cottage 2 the only one left of the six buildings opened in 1897: a Georgian Revival dormitory of brick with dressed sandstone sills, caps and belt courses, drawn by John Charles and built by Thomas Wooley of La Crosse. The only original farm building left is a board barn of about 1902 to 1908. The administration building of 1901 and 1902, red pressed brick and Colfax cut stone in a Greek cross plan by the Milwaukee firm Van Ryn and De Gelleke, was pulled down in 2021. A state historical marker (number 330, 1996) tells the story.",
  '','The marker stands on Forest Avenue just north of East Park Avenue (County Highway J). The center is state land; what it is used for now was not checked. Look from the public roads.',
  [['HMdb: Northern Wisconsin Center for the Developmentally Disabled (marker 330)','https://www.hmdb.org/m.asp?m=13297'],['Wisconsin Historical Society: Administration Building (demolished 2021)','https://wisconsinhistory.org/Records/Property/HI3264'],['Wisconsin Historical Society: Cottage 2','https://www.wisconsinhistory.org/Records/Property/HI3092'],['Wisconsin Historical Society: the barn','https://www.wisconsinhistory.org/Records/Property/HI40198']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='John Charles',builder='Thomas Wooley',archw="Wisconsin Historical Society record for Cottage 2: architect John Charles, builder Thomas Wooley of La Crosse (the demolished administration building was by Van Ryn and De Gelleke)"),
N('southern-wisconsin-home-union-grove-wi','Southern Wisconsin Home for the Feeble-Minded and Epileptic (Southern Wisconsin Center)','Union Grove','wi','inst','standing',42.69379,-88.07927,'site','view','drive','','1918–',
  "A second state home, voted in 1913 with $300,000 because most of those sent to Chippewa Falls came from the south of the state. The state bought 519 acres of the Crane estate two miles northwest of Union Grove, and the Milwaukee firm of Robert A. Messmer and Brother drew the plans after touring the most modern institutions in the country. Inmates of the state prison at Waupun built the first cottage, begun in 1918, and it opened on February 14, 1919, with 59 children; building went on into 1941. It is now the Southern Wisconsin Center, run by the Wisconsin Department of Health Services for adults with intellectual disabilities.",
  "Cream brick walls on red brick foundations, with red brick trim and red tile or shingled hipped and jerkinhead roofs, in Italian Renaissance and Mission revival styles (National Register form, 1991). The cottages stand in linked pairs forming H plans round wide lawns, among mature trees and curving drives; Cottage 2 (1918 and 1919) is the oldest. The superintendent's house of 1919 is white stucco. One pair of cottages has been razed.",
  '','21425 Spring Street, an active state center: look from the public road only.',
  [NR('91001394','Southern Wisconsin Home Historic District'),['Wisconsin Department of Health Services: Southern Wisconsin Center','https://www.dhs.wisconsin.gov/swc/index.htm']],
  brick='some',bcol='mixed',arch='Robert A. Messmer and Brother',archw="National Register form: 'The firm of Robert A. Messmer and Brother of Milwaukee was chosen as architects'"),
N('central-state-hospital-waupun-wi','Central State Hospital for the Criminally Insane (Dodge Correctional Institution)','Waupun','wi','inst','standing',43.62328,-88.73967,'site','view','drive','','1913–1977',
  "Wisconsin's hospital for the criminally insane, built beside the state prison at Waupun so that it could share the prison's power plant; prisoners dug the foundations. Foeller and Schober of Green Bay designed it in the Prairie School manner and set out to avoid the look of a prison. The main corridor and its tunnel were built in 1913, the first wings in 1914, and the first patients came on July 12, 1914; more wings followed in 1918, 1923 and 1931. In 1977 it was turned into the Dodge Correctional Institution, a maximum-security prison that took its first inmates in 1978 and was more than doubled in size from 1993.",
  "Two-storey wings of red brick with concrete sills, cornices and corner medallions, set off a single long corridor, behind a security wall of 1913; the corridor inside keeps its terrazzo floors and beige glazed tile. Later additions of the 1950s to 1990s surround much of it. On the National Register as a historic district since 1991.",
  '','An active maximum-security state prison, 1 West Lincoln Street: look from Lincoln Street only.',
  [NR('91001395','Central State Hospital Historic District'),W('Dodge Correctional Institution')],
  brick='yes',bcol='red',arch='Foeller and Schober',archw="National Register record and form: architect/builder Foeller & Schober"),
N('milwaukee-county-asylum-grounds-wauwatosa-wi','Milwaukee County Asylum grounds (Sanctuary Woods, County Grounds Park)','Wauwatosa','wi','inst','traces',43.04815,-88.02462,'area','public','walk','','1880–c. 1994',
  "Milwaukee County opened its asylum for the insane here in 1880 (the Cultural Landscape Foundation; WUWM gives 1888 for the hospital for the chronically insane), later called the Milwaukee County Hospital for the Insane, part of the county grounds that also held a poor farm, a children's home and an agricultural school. Under an early superintendent, Dr. Moses White, patients helped build a landscape of gardens: an artificial lake and waterfall, sunken gardens and a women's grove. The last asylum building came down about 1994 and is a parking lot; the county's mental health complex replaced it. The architect is given as Henry Koch in one place and E. Townsend Mix in another of the same source.",
  "In the woods: two fieldstone staircases of the women's grove, built by patients in the early 1900s, leading down into a ravine with a creek and footbridges; parts of the sunken gardens with two waterfalls, cement porches and the terraces; a walled patio and old curbed paths. A six-foot stone urn of 1908 made at the hospital stands in the Ronald McDonald House parking lot on Watertown Plank Road just south. Two cemeteries adjoin, the Poor Farm Cemetery and the Asylum Cemetery, where about 200 are believed buried in unknown places.",
  '','County Grounds Park, Milwaukee County Parks: open to walkers, entrances on 87th Street north of Watertown Plank Road and on Discovery Parkway south of Swan Boulevard (WUWM). The stairs are worn; volunteers have cleared them. Park hours not checked.',
  [['WUWM: the artifacts of a former hospital grounds (2023)','https://www.wuwm.com/2023-01-24/explore-the-artifacts-of-this-outdoor-museum-that-was-formerly-the-grounds-of-the-hospital-for-insane'],['The Cultural Landscape Foundation: Sanctuary Woods','https://www.tclf.org/sites/default/files/microsites/landslide2017/sanctuary-woods.html']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no'),
N('northern-hospital-insane-winnebago-wi','Northern Hospital for the Insane (Winnebago State Hospital)','Oshkosh','wi','inst','gone',44.07492,-88.51932,'area','view','drive','','1871–',
  "Wisconsin's second state hospital, on the shore of Lake Winnebago north of Oshkosh, which won it from Green Bay in 1870. Stephen Vaughn Shipman designed it on the Kirkbride plan, like his hospital at Mendota; building began in 1871, the first patient came on April 21, 1873, and the main building was finished on November 11, 1875, with 500 beds. It was renamed Winnebago State Hospital about the 1930s and Winnebago Mental Health Institute about the 1970s, and is still a state psychiatric hospital. The Kirkbride building was pulled down bit by bit in the 1950s and 1960s, as new buildings went up (Wikipedia).",
  "The Kirkbride building is gone; it stood just east of where Butler Avenue meets Main Street on the hospital grounds. The hospital cemetery, used from 1872 to 1973 says a sign there, lies southeast of the hospital on Asylum Point; few graves are marked, some by flat numbered stones. KirkbrideBuildings.com reports the Julaine Farrow Museum, named for a nurse who wrote the hospital's history, in the former superintendent's house. The point and Asylum Bay still carry the old name, and Winnebago County's Asylum Point Park is on the shore.",
  '','An active state hospital of the Wisconsin Department of Health Services, 1300 South Drive: look from the public roads. When the museum opens was not checked.',
  [W('Winnebago Mental Health Institute'),[KB+'Winnebago State Hospital','https://www.kirkbridebuildings.com/buildings/oshkosh/'],['Winnebago County: Asylum Point','https://www.co.winnebago.wi.us/sites/default/files/uploaded-files/asylum_point.pdf']],
  brick='?',arch='Stephen Vaughn Shipman',archw="KirkbrideBuildings.com and Wikipedia's Mendota article name Stephen Vaughn Shipman as the designer of the Kirkbride building"),
# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Minnesota
N('st-peter-state-hospital-center-building-mn','St. Peter State Hospital Center Building (Minnesota Hospital for the Insane; St. Peter State Hospital Museum)','St. Peter','mn','inst','standing',44.305416,-93.977783,'exact','open','drive','','1867–',
  "Minnesota's first state hospital for the insane, voted in 1866; St. Peter bought a 210-acre farm and lent it to the state, and the first patient came on December 6, 1866. Samuel Sloan designed it on the Kirkbride plan, with Thomas Kirkbride himself consulting, and it was built from 1867 to 1878; a fire destroyed the men's wing on November 15, 1880. The Asylum for Dangerous Insane opened on the campus in 1911, renamed the Minnesota Security Hospital in 1957. The patient wings were pulled down in the late 1960s (Wikipedia, SAH Archipedia), leaving the Center Building, the old administration block. The campus is now the St. Peter Regional Treatment Center, a secure hospital run by Minnesota's Direct Care and Treatment.",
  "A limestone building (Mankato Free Press group, 2026), the oldest part of the hospital, with a tablet bearing the date 1866 in the front pediment. It holds the St. Peter State Hospital Museum: the work of nurses and doctors, daily routine, instruments, art made in the hospital and photographs. Wikipedia dates the museum to 2006, the 2026 article to about the centennial of 1966. On the National Register since 1986.",
  '','100 Freeman Drive, on the grounds of an active secure treatment center. The museum is shown by appointment only: telephone 507-985-2249 (New Ulm Journal, 2026). Otherwise look from the public road.',
  [W('St. Peter State Hospital Museum'),W('Minnesota Security Hospital'),NR('86002117','Center Building, Minnesota Hospital for the Insane'),['New Ulm Journal: first psychiatric facility in Minnesota at 160 years (2026)','https://www.nujournal.com/news/local-news/2026/05/12/first-psychiatric-facility-in-mn-at-160-years/']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',hours='By appointment only',arch='Samuel Sloan',archw="National Register record: architects Samuel Sloan and Dr. Thomas Kirkbride; Wikipedia: 'designed by architect Samuel Sloan'",
  stone='1866',stonew="Wikipedia's article on the museum: 'The front pediment bears a tablet with the date \"1866\"'"),
N('fergus-falls-state-hospital-mn','Fergus Falls State Hospital (Third Minnesota State Hospital for the Insane, the Kirkbride)','Fergus Falls','mn','inst','standing',46.299394,-96.081313,'exact','view','drive','','1888–2005',
  "Commissioned in 1885 as the Third Minnesota State Hospital for the Insane, north of the Twin Cities to relieve St. Peter and Rochester; Fergus Falls was chosen on December 14, 1886. Warren B. Dunnell designed it on a version of the Kirkbride plan with its wings set back at slight angles in a shallow U, one of the last Kirkbride buildings in the country. SAH Archipedia dates the building to 1888 to 1906; Wikipedia says only the West Detached Ward was ready when the first patients came on July 29, 1890, and the centre and other wings were done by 1912. By the late 1920s it held about 1,700 patients, the most in the state. It became a regional treatment centre in 1971 and 1985 and closed in 2005, and the city bought it in 2007.",
  "SAH Archipedia calls it the last complete Kirkbride building in Minnesota, of smooth white stone with maroon accents on the towers of the centre building, in a Beaux-Arts manner with Romanesque round-arched windows. After years of failed plans the city sought money to pull most of it down; the state gave $3.5 million in 2018 and the 1950s administration building was demolished that May, while a developer hoped to reuse the East and West Detached wards (Perham Focus) and senior housing was proposed for the wings (KVRR, 2018). An artists' residency used the former nurses' dormitory from 2014. What has been done since was not checked. On the National Register since 1986.",
  '','1400 North Union Avenue, owned by the city of Fergus Falls. The grounds, called Kirkbride Park, have held public events (2026); the building is closed: look from the grounds and street.',
  [W('Fergus Falls Regional Treatment Center'),['SAH Archipedia: Fergus Falls Regional Treatment Center','https://archipediavm.upress.virginia.edu/buildings/MN-01-111-0010'],['Perham Focus: historic status not affected by demolition (2018)','https://www.perhamfocus.com/news/kirkbrides-historic-status-not-impacted-by-demolition'],['KVRR: wings could become senior apartments (2018)','https://www.kvrr.com/2018/03/20/wings-of-the-kirkbride-could-become-senior-apartment-homes/'],['MNopedia: Fergus Falls State Hospital','https://www.mnhs.org/mnopedia/search/index/structure/fergus-falls-state-hospital']],
  conf='mixed',brick='no',arch='Warren B. Dunnell',archw="Wikipedia and SAH Archipedia: designed by Warren B. Dunnell, adapting Kirkbride's linear plan"),
N('rochester-state-hospital-quarry-hill-mn','Rochester State Hospital cemetery and caves (Quarry Hill Park)','Rochester','mn','inst','traces',44.02919,-92.43713,'site','public','walk','','1877–1982',
  "Begun in 1877 as the Minnesota Inebriate Asylum, paid for by a tax on liquor dealers; in 1878 the legislature turned it into the Second Minnesota Hospital for the Insane, and it opened on January 1, 1879, with 100 men from St. Peter. It was built on the Kirkbride plan and renamed Rochester State Hospital in 1893. From 1948 newer buildings took the patients and by 1964 the Kirkbride buildings were torn down; the hospital closed in June 1982. In 1965 the city bought 212 acres of the hospital farm for Quarry Hill Park, and in 1984 a federal prison hospital, the Federal Medical Center, took over part of the main grounds (MNopedia).",
  "In the park: the hospital cemetery, used from 1886, with 2,019 platted graves, where volunteers have set some 500 headstones since 2007; and caves dug in 1882 by six patients into the St. Peter sandstone of the hillside as root cellars for the hospital farm's crops, with words carved on a wall by the middle entrance still to be read (Quarry Hill Nature Center).",
  '','Quarry Hill Park, City of Rochester, with the Quarry Hill Nature Center at 701 Silver Creek Road NE. Whether the caves are open to walk into was not checked; park hours not checked. The old hospital grounds to the south hold the Federal Medical Center, a federal prison: keep off.',
  [['MNopedia: Rochester State Hospital','https://mnopedia.org/structure/rochester-state-hospital'],['Quarry Hill Nature Center: history','https://qhnc.org/history']],
  brick='no'),
N('anoka-state-hospital-mn','Anoka State Hospital (Anoka State Asylum)','Anoka','mn','inst','standing',45.21528,-93.38083,'area','view','drive','','1899–1999',
  "A hospital for the state's chronic patients, the first in Minnesota built on the cottage plan, to feel less like an institution. Building began in June 1899 on about 650 acres along the Rum River to plans by the state architect, Clarence H. Johnston, Sr., and the Anoka State Asylum for the Insane opened when 100 men came from St. Peter on March 14, 1900; women came from 1906. By 1917 it had ten cottages, an auditorium and a new administration building in a half circle, linked by tunnels, for 900 patients. It was renamed Anoka State Hospital in 1937 and Anoka Metro Regional Treatment Center in 1985; the last patients moved to a new secure hospital in 1999 and the state gave the old campus to Anoka County in 2000.",
  "MNopedia calls it one of the finest examples of the cottage plan in Minnesota. Three empty cottages on Fourth Avenue, in disrepair and facing demolition in 2014 (Star Tribune), were renovated by volunteers as housing for veterans in 2017 and a fourth in 2019 (MNopedia, which elsewhere says two); as of 2020 the county's corrections department used the remaining buildings. Many of the first patients were buried on the grounds in numbered graves (Wikipedia).",
  '','Anoka County land on Fourth Avenue near the Rum River, a few blocks from the Northstar station; housing and county corrections use: look from the street.',
  [['MNopedia: Anoka State Hospital','https://mnopedia.org/structure/anoka-state-hospital'],W('Anoka Metro Regional Treatment Center'),['Star Tribune: veteran housing could take over old cottages (2014)','https://www.startribune.com/veteran-housing-could-take-over-old-cottages-on-site-of-former-anoka-state-hospital/277623981']],
  conf='mixed',brick='?',arch='Clarence H. Johnston, Sr.',archw="MNopedia: Clarence H. Johnston, Sr., the state architect for the Board of Control, designed the plans"),
N('willmar-state-hospital-mn','Willmar State Hospital (Willmar Hospital Farm for Inebriates)','Willmar','mn','inst','standing',45.14157,-95.01934,'exact','view','drive','','1912–',
  "Voted in April 1907 as a Hospital Farm for Inebriates, paid for by a 2 percent tax on liquor licences, which bought 482 acres of the Tallman Stock Farm on a lake shore in 1909. Clarence H. Johnston, Sr. laid it out on the cottage plan from 1910, and it opened on December 26, 1912, for fifty patients. It became the Willmar State Asylum in 1917 and Willmar State Hospital in 1937, and grew to nearly 1,500 patients as building went on to 1933. The farm buildings were pulled down in 1969. In 2006 two firms bought 37 buildings on about 95 acres for the MinnWest Technology Campus, and Kandiyohi County took the other seven for youth and addiction programmes.",
  "Nineteen buildings of 1912 to 1933 (National Register, 1986) in a Mediterranean or Spanish Colonial revival manner: stucco walls over a brick base and trim, and red ceramic tile roofs with cupolas, laid out along two paths from the Administration Building, which has a smooth limestone entrance and loggias joining its wings.",
  '','The Administration Building is at 1700 Technology Drive NE; the campus is offices and county programmes. Look from the campus roads; the buildings are not open to visitors as such.',
  [['MNopedia: Willmar State Hospital','https://www.mnhs.org/mnopedia/search/index/place/willmar-state-hospital'],NR('86001535','Willmar Hospital Farm for Inebriates Historic District')],
  brick='?',arch='Clarence H. Johnston, Sr.',archw="National Register record and MNopedia: designed by Clarence H. Johnston, Sr., built 1912 to 1933"),
N('moose-lake-state-hospital-mn','Moose Lake State Hospital (Minnesota Correctional Facility, Moose Lake)','Moose Lake','mn','inst','standing',46.44026,-92.758478,'area','view','drive','','1936–',
  "Minnesota's fourth hospital for the insane, for the northeast of the state: a commission chose land by the village of Moose Lake in 1935, and the hospital was built from November 1936 with a Public Works Administration grant, at a cost of $2,181,500. It took its first patients, from Fergus Falls and St. Peter, on May 2, 1938, and opened formally on June 11. It was planned as a community of its own, with an administration building, a medical centre, receiving wards, four dormitories, a nurses' home, a power house and a farm, and two more buildings were added in 1949. As the Moose Lake Regional Treatment Center it began to be turned into a state prison in 1988.",
  "Massive brick buildings in a plain Colonial Revival manner (Placeography). The colour of the brick and which of the 1938 buildings still stand were not checked.",
  '','1000 Lakeshore Drive, a medium-security state prison of the Minnesota Department of Corrections: look from the public road only.',
  [['Placeography: Moose Lake State Hospital','https://www.placeography.net/Moose_Lake_State_Hospital,_Moose_Lake,_Minnesota'],['Living New Deal: Moose Lake','https://livingnewdeal.org/projects/minnesota-correctional-facility-moose-lake-mn/'],['Minnesota Department of Corrections: Moose Lake','https://mn.gov/doc/facilities/moose-lake']],
  brick='?'),
]
